Q: NX how to use an interface library in frontend and backend A: <p>You can generate a library for interfaces in an Nx workspace using the following command:</p>
<pre class="lang-bash prettyprint-override"><code>npx nx generate @nrwl/js:library lib-interfaces
</code></pre>
<p>It will ask you about the testing library you want to use and how you want to build it. Usually <code>jest</code> and <code>tsc</code> is what you need.</p>
<p>You can also run the command with the flat <code>--dry-run</code> to test the output without affecting your repository.</p>
<p>When you create a library, the root <code>tsconfig.base.json</code> file is updated, particularly the <code>path</code> object inside the <code>compilerOptions</code> key.</p>
<p>You will find a new line like the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-json prettyprint-override"><code>{
  &quot;compilerOptions&quot;: {
    &quot;paths&quot;:{
        &quot;@organization-name/api-interfaces&quot;: [&quot;libs/api-interfaces/src/index.ts&quot;]
        }
    }
}
</code></pre>
<p>So you can use it like the following:</p>
<pre class="lang-js prettyprint-override"><code>import {Whatever} from '@organization-name/api-interfaces';
</code></pre>

Q: Insert variables in a do loop A: <p>I think you mean can you use a variable list in that form?  The answer is no.</p>
<p>You can use a colon to make a variable list of variables whose names start with the same string.  So a statement like:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>array t type: ;
</code></pre>
<p>Would find variables like TYPE1_1 and TYPE2_3.  They would appear in the list in the order they were defined to the data step (say from reading an existing dataset that contained them).</p>
<p>You might be able to do what you want if you define a multiple dimension array that has all of the variables and then just loop over the dimension you want.  But you will need to list all of the variables.  So an array defined like this:</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>array t [4,20] type1__1 - type1__20
               type2__1 - type2__20
               type3__1 - type3__20
               type4__1 - type3__20
;
</code></pre>
<p>You could then use 2 as the second index value and loop over the first index value to reference the variables you wanted: type1__2, type2__2, ...</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>do row=1 to 4;
  put row= 'Value=' t[row,2] ;
end;
</code></pre>
<p>If those do not work then you will need to use some code generation to generate the list of variable names in the order you need.</p>
<p>For example,  if you enclose your code in a macro definition then you could use a %DO loop to generate code that references the variables in the order you request.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>array t 
%do i=1 to &amp;n ;
  test&amp;i.__2
%end;
;
</code></pre>
<p>If the variables already exist in a dataset then you can query the metadata for that dataset and select the names from there into a macro variable that you could later use to place the list of variable names into your code.</p>
<pre class="lang-none prettyprint-override"><code>proc sql noprint;
select name into :varlist
from dictionary.columns
where libname='WORK' and memname='HAVE'
  and upcase(name) like 'TEST%^_^_2' escape '^'
  and 1 &lt;= input(scan(substr(name,5),1,'_'),?32.) &lt;= &amp;n
;
quit;
...
array t &amp;varlist;
...
</code></pre>
